answer:
Here is a bold, brutalist layout concept that leans into the flaws of your source material. This design uses "Acid Graphics" principles: high contrast, noise, and aggressive layering.

### The Concept: "The Glitch Stack"
Instead of fixing the dark photos, we are going to treat them as texture. We will sandwich the grainy image between a **Noise Overlay** and **Neon Blocks** to create a "vibrating" visual effect.

### The Color Palette (Gritty Neon)
*   **Void Black (Base):** `#0F0F0F` (Use this for the background to deepen the shadows of your dark photos)
*   **Acid Green (Highlight):** `#CCFF00` (Use for main date/title)
*   **Electric Pink (Accent):** `#FF00FF` (Use for bands/secondary info)
*   **Cyber Blue (Deep):** `#0000FF` (Use for shapes behind images)
*   **Grain Grey:** `#808080` (For noise transparency)

### The Code (HTML/CSS/SVG)

Copy the code below into a single `.html` file to view the proof.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <title>Gritty Neon Poster</title>
    <style>
        /* IMPORTS */
        @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Archivo+Black&family=Space+Mono:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&display=swap');

        /* RESET & SETUP */
        body { margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #111; display: flex; justify-content: center; align-items: center; min-height: 100vh; }

        .poster-container {
            width: 600px; /* Adjust based on print size */
            height: 848px; /* A3 Ratio approx */
            background-color: #0F0F0F;
            position: relative;
            overflow: hidden;
            box-shadow: 0 0 50px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
            font-family: 'Archivo Black', sans-serif;
        }

        /* LAYER 1: THE GRAINY PHOTO 
           Put your image in the src below. We force it dark and grainy via CSS filters */
        .photo-bg {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%;
            object-fit: cover;
            opacity: 0.6;
            filter: contrast(1.4) saturate(1.2) grayscale(0.3);
            mix-blend-mode: hard-light;
        }

        /* LAYER 2: SVG NOISE OVERLAY 
           This unifies the grain */
        .noise-overlay {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%;
            pointer-events: none;
            opacity: 0.15;
            z-index: 10;
        }

        /* LAYER 3: GEOMETRIC BLOCKS */
        .neon-block {
            position: absolute;
            width: 150%;
            height: 20%;
            background: #FF00FF;
            top: 65%;
            left: -20%;
            transform: rotate(-5deg);
            opacity: 0.8;
            mix-blend-mode: exclusion; /* Crucial for that neon-on-dark look */
            filter: blur(2px);
        }

        .cyber-shape {
            position: absolute;
            top: 20%;
            right: -10%;
            width: 300px;
            height: 300px;
            background: #0000FF;
            border-radius: 50%;
            opacity: 0.5;
            mix-blend-mode: screen;
            filter: blur(40px);
        }

        /* LAYER 4: TYPOGRAPHY */
        .type-stack {
            position: absolute;
            z-index: 20;
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
        }

        /* Big Title */
        h1.hero-text {
            font-size: 140px;
            line-height: 0.8;
            color: transparent;
            -webkit-text-stroke: 4px #CCFF00;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            position: absolute;
            top: 15%;
            left: 50%;
            transform: translateX(-50%) skewX(-10deg);
            white-space: nowrap;
            z-index: 30;
        }

        /* Subtitle overlapping the title */
        h2.sub-hero {
            font-size: 140px;
            line-height: 0.8;
            color: rgba(204, 255, 0, 0.2);
            position: absolute;
            top: 22%;
            left: 50%;
            transform: translateX(-50%) skewX(-10deg);
            text-transform: uppercase;
            z-index: 29;
        }

        /* Info Box */
        .info-panel {
            position: absolute;
            bottom: 5%;
            left: 5%;
            width: 80%;
            border: 2px solid #fff;
            background: rgba(0,0,0,0.8);
            backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
            padding: 20px;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: space-between;
            align-items: flex-end;
        }

        .venue {
            font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
            color: #fff;
            font-size: 1.5rem;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            border-bottom: 4px solid #FF00FF;
            padding-bottom: 10px;
        }

        .date {
            font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
            color: #CCFF00;
            font-size: 3rem;
            font-weight: 700;
        }

        .tickets {
            font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
            color: #fff;
            font-size: 0.9rem;
            background: #FF00FF;
            color: #000;
            padding: 5px 10px;
            transform: rotate(-2deg);
        }

        /* Decorative barcode line */
        .barcode {
            position: absolute;
            bottom: 0;
            right: 0;
            height: 100px;
            width: 15px;
            background: repeating-linear-gradient(
                90deg,
                #fff,
                #fff 2px,
                transparent 2px,
                transparent 4px
            );
            opacity: 0.5;
        }

    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="poster-container">
        <!-- 1. PHOTO LAYER -->
        <!-- REPLACE 'your-image.jpg' with your grainy file -->
        <img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1574391884720-3850c3d381a7?q=80&w=1000&auto=format&fit=crop" class="photo-bg" alt="Grainy Concert Photo">

        <!-- 2. SHAPES -->
        <div class="cyber-shape"></div>
        <div class="neon-block"></div>

        <!-- 3. SVG NOISE FILTER (Hidden in HTML, applied via CSS) -->
        <svg class="noise-overlay">
            <filter id="noiseFilter">
                <feTurbulence type="fractalNoise" baseFrequency="0.65" numOctaves="3" stitchTiles="stitch"/>
            </filter>
            <rect width="100%" height="100%" filter="url(#noiseFilter)"/>
        </svg>

        <!-- 4. TYPOGRAPHY -->
        <div class="type-stack">
            <!-- Duplicate text for the 'echo' effect -->
            <h2 class="sub-hero">THE SHOW</h2>
            <h1 class="hero-text">THE SHOW</h1>

            <div class="info-panel">
                <div class="venue">
                    The Basement<br>
                    <span style="font-size: 0.8em; opacity: 0.7;">Support by: Noise Machine</span>
                </div>
                <div class="tickets">TICKETS $15</div>
                <div class="date">NOV 12</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="barcode"></div>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

### Why this works for "Gritty Neon"

1.  **Hard Light Mix-Blend-Mode:** Look at the CSS `.photo-bg`. `mix-blend-mode: hard-light` takes your underexposed photo and punches the contrast, making the blacks blacker and the highlights pop, effectively "remastering" the bad photo directly in the browser.
2.  **The Noise Overlay:** The SVG `<feTurbulence>` creates a random grain pattern over the whole poster. This visually connects the digital text with the grainy photo so they don't look like they were pasted together.
3.  **Transparency & Echoing:** The H1/H2 stack uses `color: transparent` and `-webkit-text-stroke` for the main title. This creates a hollow, industrial feel. The duplicate H2 behind it adds that "ghosting" or "glitch" effect.
4.  **Backdrop Filter:** The `.info-panel` uses `backdrop-filter: blur(4px)`. This is vital. It blurs the chaotic background elements (the photo and neon blocks) *behind* the text, ensuring the essential info (Date/Venue) is readable despite the layout chaos.

### How to use:
1.  Save the code as an `.html` file.
2.  Replace the image URL in the `<img class="photo-bg" ...>` tag with your friend's photo.
3.  Adjust the `font-size` or colors (in the CSS variables section) if needed.

Here is a bold, high-impact layout designed specifically to embrace low-quality source material by turning the grain into an aesthetic feature.

This solution uses **CSS Grid** for a chaotic layout, **Mix-Blend-Modes** to create the neon glow effects, and an **SVG noise filter** to unify the grainy photos.

### The Design Specs

*   **Background:** Deep Void (`#080808`)
*   **Neon Green:** (`#CCFF00`)
*   **Acid Pink:** (`#FF2A6D`)
*   **Electric Blue:** (`#05D9E8`)
*   **Font:** `Archivo Black` (via Google Fonts) for maximum heaviness.

### The Code (HTML/CSS)

You can save this as an `.html` file to view the proof.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Grungy Neon Proof</title>
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin>
    <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Archivo+Black&family=Space+Grotesk:wght@700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet">

    <style>
        :root {
            --bg-color: #080808;
            --neon-green: #CCFF00;
            --acid-pink: #FF2A6D;
            --electric-blue: #05D9E8;
            --glass-white: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
        }

        * { box-sizing: border-box; }

        body {
            margin: 0;
            padding: 0;
            background-color: var(--bg-color);
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            font-family: 'Archivo Black', sans-serif;
            overflow-x: hidden;
        }

        /* MAIN CONTAINER - The Poster */
        .poster {
            width: 600px; /* Adjust as needed */
            height: 850px; /* 3:4 ratio approx */
            position: relative;
            background: #000;
            overflow: hidden;
            border: 1px solid #333;
        }

        /* NOISE TEXTURE OVERLAY 
           This adds the 'grit' to everything, hiding photo grain */
        .noise-overlay {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0; left: 0; width: 100%; height: 100%;
            pointer-events: none;
            z-index: 999;
            opacity: 0.15;
            filter: contrast(150%) brightness(100%);
            background-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg viewBox='0 0 200 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%3E%3Cfilter id='noiseFilter'%3E%3CfeTurbulence type='fractalNoise' baseFrequency='0.85' numOctaves='3' stitchTiles='stitch'/%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect width='100%25' height='100%25' filter='url(%23noiseFilter)' opacity='1'/%3E%3C/svg%3E");
        }

        /* PHOTO PLACEHOLDER */
        /* In production, replace background-image with your actual photo */
        .main-visual {
            position: absolute;
            top: 50%; left: 50%;
            transform: translate(-50%, -50%) scale(1.1);
            width: 100%; height: 100%;
            object-fit: cover;
            /* Simulated photo: Dark with contrast boost */
            filter: grayscale(100%) contrast(120%) brightness(0.6); 
            z-index: 1;
            background: linear-gradient(45deg, #111, #222);
        }

        /* TYPOGRAPHY LAYERS */
        .title-group {
            position: relative;
            z-index: 10;
            margin-top: 100px;
            mix-blend-mode: hard-light; /* Blends text into background weirdly */
        }

        h1 {
            font-size: 8rem;
            line-height: 0.85;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            color: transparent;
            -webkit-text-stroke: 2px white;
            margin: 0;
            letter-spacing: -5px;
        }

        .h1-stroke {
            position: absolute;
            top: -10px;
            left: -10px;
            color: var(--acid-pink);
            -webkit-text-stroke: 0px;
            z-index: -1;
            opacity: 0.8;
        }

        .h1-fill {
            color: var(--bg-color); /* Cutout effect */
            -webkit-text-stroke: 4px var(--neon-green);
        }

        /* SEMI-TRANSPARENT BLOCKS */
        .info-block {
            position: absolute;
            background: rgba(5, 217, 232, 0.15); /* Electric Blue tint */
            backdrop-filter: blur(4px);
            padding: 20px;
            border-left: 4px solid var(--electric-blue);
            color: var(--electric-blue);
            font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;
            z-index: 20;
            bottom: 250px;
            right: -50px;
            transform: rotate(-5deg);
            width: 280px;
            box-shadow: 10px 10px 0px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
        }

        .date-block {
            position: absolute;
            background: var(--neon-green);
            color: black;
            z-index: 25;
            bottom: 50px;
            left: -30px;
            padding: 10px 40px;
            font-size: 1.5rem;
            transform: rotate(-3deg);
        }

        /* DECORATIVE SVG SHAPES */
        .shape-circle {
            position: absolute;
            bottom: 150px;
            right: 50px;
            width: 150px;
            height: 150px;
            border: 2px solid var(--acid-pink);
            border-radius: 50%;
            z-index: 5;
            box-shadow: 0 0 15px var(--acid-pink);
        }

        .shape-stripe {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0;
            right: 20%;
            width: 40px;
            height: 100%;
            background: repeating-linear-gradient(
                0deg,
                transparent,
                transparent 19px,
                var(--neon-green) 20px
            );
            z-index: 2;
            opacity: 0.4;
        }

        /* GLITCH TEXT EFFECT */
        .sub-head {
            font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif;
            text-align: right;
            color: white;
            position: absolute;
            top: 40px;
            right: 40px;
            z-index: 10;
            text-transform: uppercase;
            letter-spacing: 2px;
        }

    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="poster">
        <!-- SVG Noise Filter for Texture -->
        <div class="noise-overlay"></div>

        <!-- Decorative Stripe -->
        <div class="shape-stripe"></div>
        <div class="shape-circle"></div>

        <!-- Photo Container (Replace background with <img> tag in production) -->
        <div class="main-visual">
            <!-- Ideally place an <img src="concert.jpg"> here. 
                 CSS filter grayscale/contrast handles the underexposure automatically. -->
        </div>

        <!-- Header Typography -->
        <div class="sub-head">
            Live at<br>
            <span style="color:var(--neon-green)">The Void</span>
        </div>

        <div class="title-group">
            <h1>
                <span class="h1-stroke">NEON</span>
            </h1>
            <h1 style="margin-left: 20px;">
                <span class="h1-fill">DREAM</span>
            </h1>
        </div>

        <!-- Overlapping Info Blocks -->
        <div class="info-block">
            <h2 style="margin:0; font-size:1.2rem;">OCT 14</h2>
            <p style="margin:5px 0 0 0; font-size: 0.9rem; opacity: 0.8;">DOORS: 9PM // 21+</p>
            <div style="margin-top: 10px; border-top: 1px solid white; padding-top:5px; font-weight:bold;">
                w/ THE GLITCHES
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="date-block">
            SATURDAY
        </div>

    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

### Why this works for your problem:

1.  **The "Noise" SVG:** I included a base64 SVG noise filter (`.noise-overlay`) that sits on top of everything. This creates a unified texture. If your photo is grainy, this overlay makes it look intentional. If the photo is dark, the grain gives it depth.
2.  **Photo Handling:** In the CSS for `.main-visual`, I added `filter: grayscale(100%) contrast(120%) brightness(0.6);`.
    *   *Why:* This forces your underexposed, grainy photo into black and white, then boosts contrast. It makes the photo recede into the background so the neon text pops on top without clashing with the muddy colors of the original photo.
3.  **Legibility Breaking:** The text uses `mix-blend-mode: hard-light` and overlapping spans. The word "NEON" sits slightly offset behind "DREAM". It forces the viewer to scan the image rather than read it linearly, which fits the indie/experimental vibe.
4.  **Semi-Transparent Blocks:** The `.info-block` uses `backdrop-filter: blur(4px)`. This frosted glass effect works beautifully over grainy photos because it blurs the bad details underneath while keeping the text sharp.

Here is a blueprint for a **"Brutalist Night-Vision"** style poster. This design leans into the grain and darkness of your source photos rather than fighting it. It uses CSS blend modes to create interaction between your type and images, giving it that "semi-transparent" industrial feel.

### The Palette (Gritty Neon)
Use these exact hex values. The background is not pure black, but a deep, muddy purple-grey which makes neon colors vibrate harder.

*   **Deep Void:** `#0a0510` (Background base)
*   **Acid Bleach:** `#E8F8C5` (Main text)
*   **Toxic Pink:** `#FF0066` (Accent blocks)
*   **Electric Blue:** `#3355FF` (Secondary text/Overlays)
*   **Dirty Grid:** `#383838` (Borders/lines)

### The Concept
We will layer a semi-transparent "sticker" effect over your dark photo. The text will sit *behind* some blocks and *in front* of others, using `mix-blend-mode: difference` to invert the colors wherever they overlap.

### The Code (HTML/CSS)

Copy this into a single HTML file to test. You can swap the placeholder image for your actual concert photo.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<style>
    @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Anton&family=Space+Mono:ital,wght@0,700;1,700&display=swap');

    :root {
        --bg: #0a0510;
        --acid: #E8F8C5;
        --neon-pink: #FF0066;
        --electric-blue: #3355FF;
        --glass: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08);
    }

    body { margin: 0; padding: 40px; background: #111; display: flex; justify-content: center; }

    /* POSTER CONTAINER */
    .poster {
        width: 600px;
        aspect-ratio: 2 / 3;
        background-color: var(--bg);
        position: relative;
        overflow: hidden;
        font-family: 'Anton', sans-serif;
        box-shadow: 0 20px 50px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
    }

    /* LAYER 1: THE PHOTO (Grainy Fix) */
    .photo-layer {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        background-image: url('https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1470225620780-dba8ba36b745?q=80&w=2070&auto=format&fit=crop'); /* REPLACE WITH YOUR PHOTO */
        background-size: cover;
        background-position: center;
        /* The "Gritty" Filter */
        filter: grayscale(80%) contrast(140%) brightness(60%) saturate(150%);
        z-index: 1;
    }

    /* LAYER 2: SVG NOISE OVERLAY (Makes the grain look intentional) */
    .noise-overlay {
        position: absolute;
        inset: 0;
        opacity: 0.15;
        pointer-events: none;
        z-index: 2;
        background-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg viewBox='0 0 200 200' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg'%3E%3Cfilter id='noiseFilter'%3E%3CfeTurbulence type='fractalNoise' baseFrequency='0.65' numOctaves='3' stitchTiles='stitch'/%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect width='100%25' height='100%25' filter='url(%23noiseFilter)'/%3E%3C/svg%3E");
    }

    /* LAYER 3: TYPOGRAPHY & BLOCKS */
    .content-layer {
        position: relative;
        z-index: 10;
        height: 100%;
        display: grid;
        grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr 1fr;
        padding: 20px;
    }

    /* HEADER: Huge overlapping text */
    .header {
        font-size: 14vw;
        line-height: 0.8;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        color: var(--acid);
        text-shadow: 4px 4px 0px var(--electric-blue);
        position: relative;
        transform: translateX(-10px);
    }

    .header span {
        display: block;
        mix-blend-mode: overlay;
    }

    /* MIDDLE: Semi-transparent Blocks */
    .mid-section {
        position: relative;
    }

    .block-a {
        position: absolute;
        top: 20%;
        right: 10%;
        width: 120px;
        height: 200px;
        background: var(--neon-pink);
        opacity: 0.8;
        transform: rotate(5deg);
        mix-blend-mode: hard-light; /* This is key for the vibe */
        z-index: 5;
    }

    .info-card {
        position: absolute;
        bottom: 0;
        left: 0;
        width: 80%;
        background: var(--glass);
        border: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.3);
        padding: 20px;
        backdrop-filter: blur(5px);
        font-family: 'Space Mono', monospace;
        color: white;
        font-size: 14px;
        line-height: 1.4;
        text-transform: uppercase;
        mix-blend-mode: exclusion;
    }

    /* FOOTER: Date/Venue */
    .footer {
        display: flex;
        justify-content: space-between;
        align-items: flex-end;
        color: var(--electric-blue);
        font-size: 4vw;
        text-transform: uppercase;
    }

    .venue {
        background: black;
        color: white;
        padding: 5px 15px;
        transform: skew(-10deg);
        border: 2px solid var(--electric-blue);
    }

    /* UTILS */
    .glitch-text {
        position: relative;
    }
    .glitch-text::before {
        content: attr(data-text);
        position: absolute;
        left: 2px;
        text-shadow: -2px 0 var(--neon-pink);
        top: 0;
        color: white;
        background: var(--bg);
        overflow: hidden;
        clip-path: polygon(0 0, 100% 0, 100% 45%, 0 45%);
        animation: glitch-anim 2s infinite linear alternate-reverse;
    }

    @keyframes glitch-anim {
        0% { clip-path: inset(20% 0 80% 0); }
        20% { clip-path: inset(60% 0 1% 0); }
        40% { clip-path: inset(10% 0 60% 0); }
        100% { clip-path: inset(80% 0 5% 0); }
    }

</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="poster">
    <div class="photo-layer"></div>
    <div class="noise-overlay"></div>

    <div class="content-layer">
        <div class="header">
            BAND <br>
            <span class="glitch-text" data-text="NAME">NAME</span>
        </div>

        <div class="mid-section">
            <div class="block-a"></div>
            <div class="info-card">
                <strong>Saturday, Oct 24</strong><br>
                Doors: 9pm // Set: 10:30pm<br>
                <br>
                Support by <em>The Echoes</em><br>
                Support by <em>Static Void</em>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer">
            <span>Live at</span>
            <span class="venue">The Basement</span>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

### How to Use This Layout

1.  **The Image (`filter: grayscale(80%) contrast(140%)...`):**
    *   I applied a heavy CSS filter to the `.photo-layer`. This intentionally crushes the blacks and boosts contrast, making your underexposed photos look like high-grit screen prints.
    *   **Tip:** If your photo has a strong color cast (like red lights), change the filter to `sepia(0.5) hue-rotate(200deg)` to turn it into a moody blue tone.

2.  **The Overlay (`.block-a`):**
    *   This is the neon pink square. I used `mix-blend-mode: hard-light`. This creates a chemical reaction between the pink and your photo. It creates the "semi-transparent" look you requested without just being boring opacity.

3.  **The Typography:**
    *   **Header:** Uses `Anton`. It's tall and narrow, perfect for fitting huge text over narrow images. The `mix-blend-mode: overlay` on the span makes the text interact with the background brightness.
    *   **Info Card:** Uses `Space Mono` (a technical, typewriter font). The `backdrop-filter: blur(5px)` gives it a frosted glass look, while `mix-blend-mode: exclusion` forces the text to remain visible even if it passes over the bright parts of the photo.

4.  **The SVG Noise:**
    *   I embedded a base64 SVG noise filter in `.noise-overlay`. This adds a fine layer of TV static over the entire poster, unifying the "grainy" photos with the clean vector type.

### Assembly Tips
*   **Don't center anything.** In "gritty neon" design, asymmetry creates tension. Keep the band name flush left, but the date flush right.
*   **Crop tight.** If the face in the photo is too dark, crop the image so you are seeing only the silhouette or a hand holding a guitar. Silhouettes work better with underexposed shots.
*   **Z-Index Play:** If the text is hard to read, add a semi-transparent black background (`rgba(0,0,0,0.6)`) behind the specific text line, not the whole box.

Stop worrying about the grain. **Lean into it.** Underexposed and noisy isn't a flaw—it's raw atmosphere. Safe design gets ignored. Chaotic, high-voltage design gets remembered. We aren't making a invitation; we're making a statement.

